---
// Security v2 (2026-07-03) — "Plain Words, Same Teeth" pass: this page
// is the plain-terms overview (each posture pillar leads with a plain
// sentence; the precise, falsifiable fact rides as mono small print),
// canonically deferring to /trust/security-overview for parameter-level
// depth. Fleet v2 recipes (PageHero/Section/IconTile/CtaBand, AA-safe
// accent text). The 2026-07-17 truth pass preserves the verified TLS/HSTS,
// scrypt, webhook, threat-model, and egress facts while replacing technical
// zero-access claims with the implemented LiveKit/support/retention boundary.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import IconTile from '../components/IconTile.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Security"
  description="How Driftstack protects your keys and content: encrypted connections, API keys nobody can read back, encrypted storage tied to your account, your own proxy or VPN per profile, two-factor sign-in, signed webhooks, and live sessions with no built-in way for our staff to join."
  ogImage="/og/security.png"
>
  <!-- Hero -->
  <PageHero label="Security" title="Your keys stay protected. Live sessions are not kept by default.">
    <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-8 text-tk-ink-2">
      Driftstack stores your API keys as one-way hashes and encrypts any
      credential that must stay usable, tying that encryption to your
      account so a stored credential cannot be read under a different
      account. Live-session media — the video and any audio of a running
      session — is processed only to run and stream your session and is
      not kept by default. Session records and agent transcripts are kept
      for their documented retention periods. The sections below explain
      each of these.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-sm text-tk-ink-3">
      This page is security in plain terms. The deep technical posture —
      parameter by parameter, control by control — lives at
      <a href="/trust/security-overview/" class="text-tk-accent-text underline"
        >/trust/security-overview</a
      >.
    </p>
  </PageHero>

  <!-- Six-pillar layout — each pillar: plain-language line first, the
       precise claim as mono small print, then the full paragraph. -->
  <Section label="The posture" title="Six promises, built into the architecture.">
    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-12 md:grid-cols-2">
      <!-- Pillar 1 -->
      <div>
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M2 12h20M12 2v20" /><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          01 · Transport
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold text-tk-ink">
          Everything between you and us travels encrypted.
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs text-tk-ink-3">TLS 1.2 + 1.3, HSTS preload-eligible (browsers forced onto HTTPS).</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-tk-ink-2">
          All traffic to us is HTTPS — encrypted the whole way.
          Cloudflare, our edge network, handles the encryption first
          and verifies it is really talking to our own server before
          passing anything on. Our API server accepts only TLS 1.2
          and 1.3 and tells browsers (via a 2-year HSTS header with
          <code>includeSubDomains</code> and <code>preload</code>)
          never to connect to us unencrypted. No plain-HTTP page
          exists on any customer-facing path. You identify yourself by
          sending your API key with each request — no client
          certificates to install.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Pillar 2 -->
      <div>
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M5 12h14M12 5l7 7-7 7" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          02 · Proxies
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold text-tk-ink">
          Sessions use your own proxy or VPN.
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs text-tk-ink-3">Per-profile SOCKS5, OpenVPN or WireGuard; the Test button shows what to expect from a proxy before you launch.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-tk-ink-2">
          A profile can attach a SOCKS5 proxy at a public address as its
          exit to the internet (<a href="/glossary/#egress" class="text-tk-accent-text underline">egress</a>),
          or an OpenVPN file (.ovpn) or WireGuard file (.conf) — VPN exits
          are on paid plans — so websites see that address instead of ours. Before the session starts we
          check the proxy details and that a SOCKS5 proxy can be reached
          with a real connection through it, and
          website address lookups go through the proxy
          too, so they do not reveal your real location. A VPN file is
          checked before it is accepted: Driftstack does not run scripts
          from VPN configs. An OpenVPN file that carries a script directive
          is refused with the line named; from a WireGuard file only the
          keys, addresses, endpoint, allowed IPs, DNS and MTU are read — its
          PostUp/PreUp hooks are never used. Proxies on
          private or local addresses (for example localhost or a home
          network address) are not accepted. A VPN tunnel carries
          everything the profile does, including WebRTC and HTTP/3. On a
          SOCKS5 proxy, whether WebRTC and HTTP/3 traffic can use the
          proxy depends on your proxy's UDP support: the Test button
          shows what to expect before you launch, and a running session
          shows whether HTTP/3 is actually in use. On a proxy that
          cannot carry HTTP/3, it is switched off rather than leaked. The
          desktop app launches a profile only through a proxy or VPN you
          attach, and a session created through the API names one of your
          saved proxies when it is created. Driftstack does not route your
          traffic through a shared exit of its own.
          We never store the pages your sessions visit. See
          <a href="/trust/security-overview/" class="text-tk-accent-text underline">/trust/security-overview</a>
          for the full picture.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Pillar 3 -->
      <div>
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <circle cx="9" cy="12" r="7" /><path d="M16 12h6M19 9v6" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          03 · API keys
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold text-tk-ink">
          We couldn't read your keys if we tried.
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs text-tk-ink-3">Stored only as one-way scrypt hashes — never readable.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-tk-ink-2">
          API keys are scrambled one-way with scrypt — a deliberately
          slow algorithm that makes guessing impractical — before they
          ever touch the database. The readable key is shown exactly
          once, when you create it; after that nobody at Driftstack —
          admin, support or operations — can recover it. A database
          breach would expose scrambled values, not keys. To keep
          requests fast, a key that was just checked is remembered in
          protected short-term memory for 30 seconds — speed without
          weakening how keys are stored. Keys can be rotated with a
          24-hour overlap and revoked at any time. The desktop app signs in
          with a short code you approve in the dashboard, so it receives its
          own restricted sign-in credential without a key being copied by
          hand.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Pillar 4 -->
      <div>
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M22 12h-4l-3 9L9 3l-3 9H2" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          04 · Webhooks
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold text-tk-ink">
          Every webhook proves it came from us.
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs text-tk-ink-3">Signed (HMAC-SHA256) · stale messages rejected.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-tk-ink-2">
          A webhook is a message we send to your systems when
          something happens — say, a session finishing. Every delivery
          carries a signature proving it came from us and wasn't
          altered: the X-Driftstack-Signature header,
          in the form <code class="font-mono">t=&lt;timestamp&gt;,v1=&lt;hex&gt;</code>.
          Your code checks it with one SDK helper,
          <code class="font-mono">verifyWebhookSignature</code>
          (a constant-time compare, built to resist timing tricks).
          Messages older than the default 5-minute timestamp
          tolerance are rejected, so an intercepted copy can't be
          re-sent later ("replay"); secrets rotate per-endpoint
          without touching the rest of your account.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Pillar 5 -->
      <div>
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <circle cx="12" cy="7" r="4" /><path d="M5 21v-2a4 4 0 0 1 4-4h6a4 4 0 0 1 4 4v2" /><path d="M9 7l2 2 4-4" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          05 · Team roles (RBAC)
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold text-tk-ink">
          Your whole team can look. Only admins can change.
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs text-tk-ink-3">Admin and member roles; only admins can make changes.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-tk-ink-2">
          Invite teammates by email from the desktop app or the
          dashboard; each invitation link works for seven days. Members
          can view the owner's profiles, past sessions, audit log and
          settings, but only admins can change anything or work in a
          live session (role-based access control). Sessions, profiles,
          webhooks, API keys, email preferences — every change is
          permission-checked before it runs. Audit entries record who
          acted (the member) separately from whose account it was (the
          owner). Owners can review recorded activity in the dashboard's
          audit log, via
          <code class="font-mono">/v1/account/audit-log</code>, or as a
          CSV export.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Pillar 6 — full width -->
      <div class="md:col-span-2">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M12 2L4 5v6c0 5.5 3.8 10.7 8 11.8 4.2-1.1 8-6.3 8-11.8V5l-8-3z" /><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          06 · Live-media handling
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold text-tk-ink">
          Live-session media is not retained by default.
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs text-tk-ink-3">
          Live session media is encrypted in transit (DTLS-SRTP); there is no built-in way for Driftstack staff to join your session.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-tk-ink-2">
          Driftstack keeps license details, session records (id, status,
          timestamps) and aggregate usage counts. Live-session streams
          are encrypted in transit, used to deliver the session to you,
          and dropped when the session ends. Driftstack staff have no
          built-in way to join a customer's live session. Screenshots,
          page snapshots and PDFs you request through the sessions API are
          returned directly in the API response and are not stored. In an
          AI-agent session the screenshot from each step is held in server
          memory for up to 30 minutes (at most 20 per session) so the
          desktop app can show it, then dropped; it is never written to
          disk. Your desktop recordings stay
          on your own computer and are not uploaded. For self-hosted
          deployments, even session records stay inside your network;
          only periodic "is this license still valid?" check-ins reach
          our servers.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- What we don't claim -->
  <Section label="What we don't claim" title="Honest scope." class="bg-tk-surface">
    <ul class="mt-8 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
      <li class="card bg-tk-raised p-6 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10" />
            <path d="M4.93 4.93l14.14 14.14" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <strong class="block text-tk-ink">No SOC 2.</strong>
        Driftstack is not SOC 2 certified. The current compliance basis is
        documented in the trust center; we do not imply certification through
        vague "enterprise-grade" language.
      </li>
      <li class="card bg-tk-raised p-6 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10" />
            <path d="M4.93 4.93l14.14 14.14" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <strong class="block text-tk-ink">No ISO 27001.</strong>
        Driftstack is not ISO 27001 certified. The trust center documents
        the controls and evidence currently in place.
      </li>
      <li class="card bg-tk-raised p-6 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <rect x="3" y="4" width="18" height="16" rx="2" />
            <path d="M3 9h18M9 4v16" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <strong class="block text-tk-ink">Sub-processors are published.</strong>
        The live register lists every provider with its purpose, region,
        and transfer mechanism, including conditional services. Read it at
        <a href="/trust/sub-processors/" class="text-tk-accent-text underline">/trust/sub-processors</a>.
      </li>
      <li class="card bg-tk-raised p-6 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M12 22s-8-4.5-8-11.8A8 8 0 0 1 12 2a8 8 0 0 1 8 8.2c0 7.3-8 11.8-8 11.8z" />
            <circle cx="12" cy="10" r="3" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <strong class="block text-tk-ink">Data residency is EU-default.</strong>
        {/* S30 2026-07-07 (founder decision: soften) — R2 dropped from
            the all-in-the-EU list: file objects live on Cloudflare R2
            in the default jurisdiction (EU + US replication). */}
        Servers and database are in the EU (Hetzner, Germany; Neon EU;
        Upstash EU). Uploaded files (avatars, for example) are on
        Cloudflare R2, which can keep copies outside the EU. Sessions
        may run outside the EU under the EU's Standard Contractual
        Clauses (SCCs) and the EU-US Data Privacy Framework. Details in
        the sub-processor list.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <!-- V-503 — defense in depth -->
  <Section
    label="Defense in depth"
    title="Layered controls. No single line of defense."
    lead="Each layer below is independently effective; an attacker would have to bypass every one to reach customer keys or session content. We assume any single layer can fail and design the next layer to survive it."
  >
    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="card p-6">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M2 12s3-7 10-7 10 7 10 7-3 7-10 7-10-7-10-7z" /><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          Edge
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-2 text-lg font-medium text-tk-ink">
          Encrypted connections, automated abuse blocked
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
          All traffic enters through Cloudflare. Cloudflare verifies
          the certificate on our own server before forwarding anything,
          so a request cannot be quietly intercepted on the way in.
          Cloudflare's firewall and bot protection block automated
          abuse before it reaches our servers.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card p-6">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <rect x="3" y="6" width="18" height="12" rx="2" /><path d="M3 10h18" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          Origin
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-2 text-lg font-medium text-tk-ink">
          A locked-down server
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
          Our server accepts only modern encrypted connections (TLS 1.2
          and 1.3 with Mozilla's recommended cipher settings, HSTS
          preload-eligible). A firewall allows only administrative
          access and web traffic, and any unencrypted web request is
          redirected to the encrypted connection. Repeat attackers have
          their address banned automatically.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card p-6">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <rect x="2" y="3" width="20" height="14" rx="2" /><path d="M8 21h8M12 17v4" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          Application
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-2 text-lg font-medium text-tk-ink">
          Three checks on every request
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
          Every authenticated request passes three checks, in order:
          is this API key real, and whose is it (auth)? Is this key
          allowed to do this specific thing (scope check — granular
          per-resource permissions like
          <code class="font-mono">read:sessions</code>)? And has this
          account sent too many requests too fast (rate limit)? A
          request that fails any check is refused on the spot — it
          never reaches the rest of the system.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card p-6">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <ellipse cx="12" cy="5" rx="9" ry="3" /><path d="M3 5v6c0 1.7 4 3 9 3s9-1.3 9-3V5" /><path d="M3 11v6c0 1.7 4 3 9 3s9-1.3 9-3v-6" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          Data
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-2 text-lg font-medium text-tk-ink">
          Encryption at rest + isolation
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
          API keys are stored as one-way hashes (scrypt, with a unique
          random salt per key), so recovering the original key is
          impossible by design. Account passwords are protected the
          same way. Secrets that must stay usable — two-factor login
          secrets, any Anthropic API key you bring for the AI agent,
          the passwords and keys of your saved proxies and VPNs, and
          the keys that authenticate desktop-app control commands —
          are stored encrypted (AES-256-GCM) under a key that exists
          only on our server, decrypted in memory only when needed and
          never written to logs. Our database and cache providers also
          encrypt everything they store (encryption at rest).
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card p-6">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z" /><path d="M14 2v6h6" /><path d="M9 13h6M9 17h4" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          Audit
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-2 text-lg font-medium text-tk-ink">
          Recorded customer audit trail
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
          Account-security and management events — API key creation,
          rotation and revocation, two-factor (MFA) enrollment, profile
          changes, webhook secret rotations, logins and password
          changes — are recorded and cannot be edited after the fact.
          Everyday use of an API key is not recorded in the audit
          trail. You can export your records as a CSV or JSON file from
          the dashboard or via
          <code class="font-mono">/v1/account/audit-log/export</code>
          (your data-portability right under GDPR Article 20).
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card p-6">
        <IconTile class="mb-3">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8" /><path d="M21 21l-4.35-4.35" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-tk-accent-text">
          Observability
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-2 text-lg font-medium text-tk-ink">
          Errors tracked, content never logged
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
          Server errors go to Sentry, our error-tracking service (EU
          region), with personal data scrubbed out first. Request logs
          record only technical details — never the body of your
          request or of our response — just enough to reconstruct what
          happened.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- V-503 — threat model summary. Bespoke section shell (not the
       Section component): the W325.B baseline pins the rendered
       `>Threat model<` label form in this file's source. -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-tk-border bg-tk-surface">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20 md:py-24">
      <p class="section-label">Threat model</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-tk-ink sm:text-3xl md:text-4xl">
        What we defend against. What we don't.
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-12 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-tk-ink">In scope</h3>
          <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-tk-accent-text">•</span>
              <span>
                <strong>API key compromise</strong> via leaked secret. Mitigation:
                keys stored only as scrypt hashes; rotation flow with
                24-hour grace (the old key keeps working for 24 hours
                so nothing breaks mid-switch); granular per-resource
                scopes mean a stolen key can only do what that key was
                allowed to do (a limited blast radius).
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-tk-accent-text">•</span>
              <span>
                <strong>Webhook signature forgery</strong>. Mitigation:
                every delivery is cryptographically signed
                (HMAC-SHA256) and messages outside the 5-minute
                timestamp tolerance are rejected; while you rotate a
                secret we sign with both old and new (dual-signing
                during the grace window), so verification never breaks.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-tk-accent-text">•</span>
              <span>
                <strong>Session hijacking</strong> via cross-site or
                cross-account access. Mitigation: every lookup is
                restricted to your own account; a probe at another
                account's data gets a plain "not found" (cross-account
                lookups return 404, never 403 — the response never even
                confirms the thing exists).
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-tk-accent-text">•</span>
              <span>
                <strong>Brute-force auth attempts</strong>. Mitigation:
                rate limits per IP address on login and API key checks;
                IP addresses that keep failing are banned automatically.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-tk-accent-text">•</span>
              <span>
                <strong>Stolen browser session token</strong> (dashboard).
                Mitigation: two-factor sign-in (authenticator app plus
                recovery codes) with a fresh code demanded before risky
                actions such as disabling it or deleting the account; an
                active-sign-ins page that lists every session and lets
                you revoke one or all of the others; sign-ins that
                expire; and a password change that signs out every other
                session.
              </span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-tk-ink">Explicitly out of scope</h3>
          <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-tk-accent-text">→</span>
              <span>
                <strong>Customer's destination response content.</strong>
                Driftstack keeps only basic session details (id, status,
                timestamps) — never the content of the pages your
                sessions visit. Whatever your code does inside a
                session is yours to secure, not ours.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-tk-accent-text">→</span>
              <span>
                <strong>Detection-vendor cat-and-mouse evolution.</strong>
                Our anti-detection work is built into the browser
                engine itself rather than added on afterwards, and is
                continuously updated; we can't promise forever-perfect
                parity against every detection vendor. See the
                <a href="/trust/cumulative-rig/" class="text-tk-accent-text underline">cumulative rig
                methodology</a> for what we check, and on which device
                profiles.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-tk-accent-text">→</span>
              <span>
                <strong>Customer's keyboard / network at the endpoint.</strong>
                We can't protect a leaked API key on a customer
                developer's laptop or a webhook secret pasted into a
                build server that leaks its settings (a misconfigured
                CI runner).
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-tk-accent-text">→</span>
              <span>
                <strong>Nation-state actors with sub-processor access.</strong>
                We assume our hosting providers (listed on the
                <a href="/trust/sub-processors/" class="text-tk-accent-text underline">sub-processors page</a>)
                operate under their published security posture; we
                don't add extra defences against government-level
                attackers on top of that.
              </span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        </div>
    </div>
  </section>

